: 1781502064:0;brew upgrade gh
: 1781502094:0;# 1. Is the install "all" or "selected"?\
gh api orgs/magic-sandbox-security/installations \\
  --jq '.installations[] | select(.app_slug|test("octo-sts")) | {id, repository_selection}'\
\
# 2. If "selected", is .github in the set? (this is the repo that 404s, not test-cookie-jar)\
gh api --paginate "/user/installations/<INSTALL_ID>/repositories" \\
  --jq '.repositories[].full_name' | grep -i '/\.github$'
: 1781502154:0;gh api --paginate "/user/installations/140365910/repositories" \\
  --jq '.repositories[].full_name' | grep -i '/\.github$'
: 1781502509:0;That settles the path question â€ƒ´ resolver reads `<org>/.github` at `.github/chainguard/<identity>.sts.yaml`, your file is there, 200 on `main`. Path is correct. So the 404 is not "wrong location."\
\
But notice what your own `gh api` 200 proves and doesn't prove: it proves the file exists and *your user token* can read it. The resolver doesn't use your token â€ƒ´ it uses the **App installation token for `magic-sandbox-security`**, scoped by `contents: read`. The only way `GetContents` returns 404 against a path that genuinely exists is that the **installation making the call can't see that repo**. GitHub's contents API returns 404 (not 403) when the authenticated identity lacks access â€ƒ´ so "not installed on `.github`" and "file doesn't exist" are indistinguishable from the client, and both surface as exactly your error inside `fetchTrustPolicyRaw`.\
\
That's the whole game now. The resolver's `GetContents(owner=magic-sandbox-security, repo=.github, ...)` is being made by an installation that does not have `.github` in its repository selection. Reinstalled 04:50:34âƒ¦ƒ²04:50:52Z, org has pre-existing internal repos, failures continued after â€ƒ´ a fresh install on an org with pre-existing repos lands with whatever selection was chosen, and if that was "Only select repositories" with `test-cookie-jar` picked but not `.github`, you get precisely this: every claim correct, file present, resolver path correct, 404 because the install can't read `.github`.\
\
The two-line confirmation, and why it's different from what you've run:\
\
```bash\
# 1. Is the install "all" or "selected"?\
gh api orgs/magic-sandbox-security/installations \\
  --jq '.installations[] | select(.app_slug|test("octo-sts")) | {id, repository_selection}'\
\
# 2. If "selected", is .github in the set? (this is the repo that 404s, not test-cookie-jar)\
gh api --paginate "/user/installations/<INSTALL_ID>/repositories" \\
  --jq '.repositories[].full_name' | grep -i '/\.github$'\
```\
\
If step 1 returns `"all"`, then the installation genuinely sees every repo and we're wrong â€ƒ´ at that point it *is* app-side and the issue report is warranted. But if step 1 is `"selected"` and step 2 comes back empty, that's your bug, full stop, and the fix is one click: **Org âƒ¦ƒ² Settings âƒ¦ƒ² GitHub Apps âƒ¦ƒ² octo-sts âƒ¦ƒ² Configure âƒ¦ƒ²** add `.github` (or switch to All repositories). Zero YAML changes â€ƒ´ consistent with "identical config works in the other org," because in that org the install includes its `.github`.\
\
The reason I keep pulling back to this rather than endorsing the escalation: every piece of evidence you've gathered (file 200, claims match, path hardcoded correct) was collected with *your* credentials or from *source*, and none of it tests the one identity that actually performs the read. The installation-token read is the missing measurement. The cleanest single test is to reproduce `GetContents` as the installation itself:\
\
```bash\
# Mint an installation token for the app on this org, then:\
curl -sS -w "\n%{http_code}\n" \\
  -H "Authorization: token <INSTALLATION_TOKEN>" \\
  -H "Accept: application/vnd.github.raw" \\
  "https://api.github.com/repos/magic-sandbox-security/.github/contents/.github/chainguard/test-cookie-jar.sts.yaml?ref=main"\
```\
\
If that 404s where your user token 200s, the issue report would be wrong and you'd have spent a maintainer round-trip on a local install-scope gap.\
\
So: before posting to #1286, run step 1. It's one call and it's dispositive. If `selected` + no `.github` âƒ¦ƒ² fix the install, done. If `all` âƒ¦ƒ² escalate, and include the installation-token read result as proof you tested the actual calling identity. Want me to draft the issue comment in the `all`-case shape so it's ready if that's where you land?
